 1 big thing: Exclusive: Americans expect a jobless AI boom 
 
 By Ben Berkowitz 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: CivicScience; Chart: Erin Davis/Axios Americans are optimistic AI will grow the economy — but not that it will create jobs in the process, per the first release of the new Axios-CivicScience AI Sentiment Index out today. 

 Why it matters: The AI boom is often criticized as a jobless one, which may explain why sentiment continues to run so negative on what is otherwise such a transformative technology. 

 The big picture: The new monthly Axios- CivicScience index tracks Americans' views across six measures: AI's effects on quality of life, society, jobs and economic growth, plus support for AI development and new data centers.

 How it works: The data was collected in online polling from Oct. 1–26, with more than 7,300 respondents ages 18+. 

 By the numbers: October's net sentiment was 41.8 on a 0–100 scale, indicating broad negativity on AI.

 Of the six components, only economic growth had a positive sentiment score. Yes, but: While economic growth net sentiment (positive responses minus negative responses) was +10, "create more jobs" sentiment was -30.4, indicating a massive gap between what people think AI will do collectively and the impact it'll have individually. 

 The grim outlook matches other recent polls, like an Axios-Generation Lab survey of young adults in August; more than a quarter said they'd lost a job to AI or knew someone who had. What's next: The index will be released the first Friday of every month starting in December. 

 
 
